fix(voice-live): cancel speaker echo so open mic stops interrupting itself

The first dogfood session interrupted itself continuously. On speakers the
microphone hears the assistant, the provider's voice activity detection
reads that as the user talking, and it barges in on its own sentence.

Cancel the echo instead of muting the microphone, which would have
defeated real barge-in. On macOS mic and speaker now run through one
VoiceProcessingIO unit, which subtracts what it rendered from what it
captured. A 32-tap windowed-sinc decimator brings its 24 kHz microphone
side down to the 16 kHz the provider wants, carrying filter state across
callbacks so chunked audio matches a single pass. This is the native
equivalent of the `echoCancellation: true` that the working browser
clients get from getUserMedia.

Also pass start and end speech sensitivity on open mic, defaulting both
to low, matching those clients.

No platform gets a self-interrupting default. Open mic is the default
only where the speaker signal can be cancelled; elsewhere `minutes talk`
defaults to push-to-talk, which never sends microphone audio while the
assistant speaks, and `--open-mic` warns that it needs headphones.
Windows and Linux compile exactly as before: the dependency sits in the
macOS target block and the module behind a macOS attribute.

